---
<Base
  title="AI Search for Doctors, Dentists and Allied Health — Trivarone"
  description="Trivarone checks whether doctors, dentists and allied health practices appear when people search for what they do, on Google, ChatGPT and AI answers."
  path="/"
  ogTitle="Trivarone — SEO and AI SEO for Doctors, Dentists and Allied Health"
  ogDescription="Your practice ranks for its own name. Does it appear when someone searches for what you actually do? A free check across Google, AI Mode, ChatGPT, Gemini and Perplexity."
>
  <script type="application/ld+json" slot="head" set:html={JSON.stringify(schema)} />

  <section class="titleblock">
    <div class="shell">
      <p class="eyebrow">SEO · Local SEO · AI SEO</p>
      <h1>Being found by the people <em>looking for you</em>.</h1>
      <p class="standfirst">
        A practice can be easy to find by name and hard to find by what it does.
        Trivarone checks both for doctors, dentists and allied health practices in
        Australia, across Google, Google's AI Mode, ChatGPT, Gemini and Perplexity,
        reports what came back, and fixes what is worth fixing.
      </p>
      <div class="actions">
        <a class="btn" href="/check/">Get the free discoverability check</a>
        <a class="btn btn--ghost" href="/services/">See what the work involves</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band">
    <div class="shell">
      <p class="section-label">The problem</p>
      <h2>Ranking first for your own name proves very little.</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p class="lede">
          Search your practice name and the listing, the reviews and the website are all
          there. That search only finds you for people who already know you exist.
        </p>
        <p>
          Now search what you do, the way a new patient would phrase it. A
          physiotherapist in a suburb. A speech pathologist taking new clients. A dentist
          near a postcode. That is the search a new patient runs.
        </p>
        <p>
          Some people ask ChatGPT, Gemini or Perplexity before they search at all. A
          practice can appear in one of those places and be missing from the rest
          without knowing.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band band--rule">
    <div class="shell">
      <p class="section-label">How it runs</p>
      <h2>Check first. Then fix what is worth fixing.</h2>

      <ol class="findings">
        <li class="finding">
          <span class="finding__no">01</span>
          <div class="finding__body">
            <h3>The check</h3>
            <p>
              Your practice is searched two ways: by name, and by what it does.
              Five places are checked: Google's ordinary results, Google's AI Mode,
              ChatGPT, Gemini and Perplexity. Where the practice sits in Google Maps is
              checked alongside them. You are told what came back, what did not, and who
              appeared instead. This part is free.
            </p>
          </div>
        </li>
        <li class="finding">
          <span class="finding__no">02</span>
          <div class="finding__body">
            <h3>What is causing it</h3>
            <p>
              You get the findings in plain words: what is happening, what effect it has,
              and what could be done about it. Some causes are free to fix. Some cannot be
              fixed at any price, and you are told that as well.
            </p>
          </div>
        </li>
        <li class="finding">
          <span class="finding__no">03</span>
          <div class="finding__body">
            <h3>The work itself</h3>
            <p>
              The listings, the wording on your homepage, and articles that answer what
              people ask before they call. Where advertising rules apply, they are flagged,
              and you review everything before it is published.
            </p>
          </div>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band band--rule band--wide">
    <div class="shell">
      <p class="section-label">Who Trivarone works with</p>
      <h2>Two groups, one shared problem.</h2>

      <div class="split">
        <div class="panel">
          <h3>Doctors and dentists</h3>
          <p>
            GPs, medical specialists and dentists in private practice.
          </p>
          <p>
            <a href="/faq/#doctors-and-dentists">See the full list of professions in the FAQ.</a>
          </p>
          <ul class="plainlist">
            <li>Searched the way patients phrase it, not the way the profession does</li>
            <li>Searches by patients and searches by referrers treated separately</li>
            <li>AHPRA advertising rules identified before any writing begins</li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <div class="panel">
          <h3>Allied health</h3>
          <p>
            Allied health practitioners in private practice.
          </p>
          <p>
            <a href="/faq/#allied-health">See the full list of professions in the FAQ.</a>
          </p>
          <ul class="plainlist">
            <li>Local search comes first: the suburb, then the condition</li>
            <li>Conditions treated, not just the name of the profession</li>
            <li>Factual, educational content with no outcome claims</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band band--navy">
    <div class="shell">
      <p class="section-label">Where Trivarone works</p>
      <h2>Based in Brisbane, working across Australia.</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p class="lede">
          The work is done online, so a practice in Perth or Hobart is served the same
          way as one in Brisbane.
        </p>
        <p>
          Enquiries from outside Australia are welcome and considered individually.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="actions">
        <a class="btn" href="/contact/">Get in touch</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
